"# Chapter 9 — Hygiene, the Motoi way: lexical scope

Hygiene, in the module sense, means one thing: **code does not read another piece of code's private state**. Every private thing has walls, and the walls hold. The other dialect enforces this at the boundary of `module`/`export`. Motoi enforces the very same property with something more fundamental and airtight: **lexical scope**. This chapter teaches hygiene in Motoi's own terms, and marks the other dialect's introspection verbs (which Motoi lacks) as a boundary.

## 9.1 What hygiene protects

The point of a private helper is that it is *your* business. When you refactor — rename a helper, restructure a table, add caching — no other code should notice, as long as the public surface still behaves the same.

Hygiene is what makes this possible. If any code could reach into any other code and grab a private variable, \"private\" would mean nothing. Refactoring would break people at a distance. Everything would become coupled by accident.

## 9.2 The hygiene rules, in Motoi

Motoi gives you these guarantees through scope:

**Rule 1: You cannot see a name that lives in another closure's private interior.** If `flip-run` hides a helper `heads-or-tails` inside itself, nothing outside can name `heads-or-tails`. It is invisible.

**Rule 2: You cannot mutate a variable you have no reference to.** A variable sealed in a closure (section 3.7's counter) can only be touched through the verbs the closure hands out. There is no back door.

**Rule 3: Redefining a name locally does not change the original.** If you `(define greet ...)` your own `greet`, you shadow the name in your scope; you do not alter anyone else's definition.

Together these make each closure a black box to every other. Here is Rule 1, proven:

```scheme
(define (flip-run count)
  (define (heads-or-tails n) (if (< n 0.5) 'heads 'tails))  ; PRIVATE
  (heads-or-tails (/ count 100.0)))

(list (flip-run 20)                            ; public verb works
      (guard (e (#t 'cannot-reach))            ; private helper is invisible
        (heads-or-tails 0.1)))
;; ⇒ (heads cannot-reach)
```

`flip-run` works; reaching for its private `heads-or-tails` from outside errors, and we catch that error to prove it is genuinely unreachable.

## 9.3 How Motoi enforces hygiene

At runtime, a private variable lives in a lexical scope that has no name in the outside world. Even if you knew the name, you would have no reference to reach it. There is no `get-private-var` verb; there is no way to escape the scope. This is stronger than an honor-system export list — it is a property of how the interpreter looks names up.

## 9.4 Why hygiene matters more than it looks like it should

If you have never worked in a language without hygiene, you might not appreciate what it buys:

- **Refactoring is safe.** Restructure a closure's interior freely; nothing outside grew a dependency on it.
- **Understanding is local.** To understand a verb, read it and its helpers. You do not grep the whole codebase for who else touches its internals.
- **Debugging is bounded.** When something goes wrong inside a verb, the fault is inside that verb or in how a caller used its public surface. No distant code was poking its private state.
- **Testing is meaningful.** You test the public surface, knowing that is the entire contract. There is no hidden back door.

All four break down without hygiene.

## 9.5 Exceptions and escapes

Motoi is not a prison, but its escapes are narrow. There is no routine way to read a closure's private state from outside — that is the whole point. In a REPL you debug by editing your own code and re-running, not by reaching past scope. There is no in-language \"peek at private state\" back door in normal execution.

## 9.6 There is no module-introspection surface

The other dialect offers verbs that read metadata *about* a module — `module-verbs`, `module-hash`, `module-version`, `module-source`, `module-imports`, `module-signature`. **Motoi has none of these**; each yields `error: unbound symbol: ...`. There are no module objects to introspect. The Motoi way to know what a piece of code offers is to read its source and its documentation, and to call its verbs — an `unbound symbol` error tells you when a name is not there.

That is a genuine trade: the other dialect's tooling (dependency analyzers, doc generators built on `module-imports`) has no in-language basis in Motoi, because Motoi has no module graph to analyze. Its dependency story is simpler: everything depends on the one standard library, which is always present.

## 9.7 Hygiene and macros

There is a second, related meaning of \"hygiene\": *macro* hygiene. When a macro references a name, hygienic macro expansion looks that name up in the *macro's* scope, not the caller's. So a macro that expands to use `if` gets the real `if`, even if the caller rebound `if` locally. Motoi's macros are hygienic in this sense. The details fill their own chapter elsewhere; here the point is that hygiene covers the whole compilation environment, not just variables.

## 9.8 Scoped mutation — a real Motoi pattern

Say you have a dynamic value — a current color — and you want to override it just for the duration of some work, then restore it. You seal the value in a closure and hand out a *scoped-override verb* that saves, sets, runs, and restores — so callers borrow the override for a scope instead of seizing the variable:

```scheme
(define with-color #f)
(define get-color #f)

(let ((color 'red))
  (set! get-color (lambda () color))
  (set! with-color
    (lambda (c thunk)
      (let ((saved color))          ; save
        (set! color c)              ; set for the scope
        (let ((result (thunk)))
          (set! color saved)        ; restore
          result)))))

(list (get-color)                                 ; default
      (with-color 'blue (lambda () (get-color)))  ; overridden inside
      (get-color))                                ; restored after
;; ⇒ (red blue red)
```

`with-color` hides the save/set/restore machinery behind one clean verb, and `color` itself is sealed in the `let` — no caller can reach it directly. That is the pattern: if you have dynamic state, hand out a scoped-mutation verb rather than exposing the variable. Hygiene stays intact — the override lasts exactly one scope, then the original value returns.

## 9.9 Try it

Write a public verb with a private helper nested inside it. Confirm the public verb works and that naming the helper from outside errors (wrap the outside call in `guard` as in section 9.2 to prove it). Then build `with-color` from section 9.8 and confirm the override is visible only inside the scope. That is hygiene in Motoi: enforced by lexical scope, no `module`/`export` required.

Chapter 10 covers lazy vs eager loading in the other dialect — and why, in a batteries-included language, everything is simply already loaded. Turn the page.")
